PASSWD(5)                   File Formats

NAME
    passwd, group, shadow - account databases

SYNOPSIS
    /etc/passwd
    /etc/group
    /etc/shadow

DESCRIPTION
    Each line of /etc/passwd describes one account in seven fields
    separated by colons.

        name:x:uid:gid:full name:home:shell

    The second field holds x, because the password hash is kept in
    /etc/shadow, which only root can read. Each line of /etc/group
    describes one group in four fields, the last of which lists the member
    accounts separated by commas.

        name:x:gid:member,member

    Each line of /etc/shadow holds the hash of one account in nine fields,
    of which minios uses the first two.

        name:hash:lastchange:min:max:warn:inactive:expire:flag

    A hash is a SHA-256 crypt string beginning with $5$. An empty hash
    means that the account has no password and logs in without a prompt.
    A hash beginning with ! or * locks the account.

    The three files live in /usr/local/etc, the package prefix on the
    data volume, and the names in /etc are symbolic links to them, which
    keeps the accounts across rebuilds of the root image.

    Lines beginning with # and lines with missing or malformed fields are
    ignored. Account and group names start with a lowercase letter and
    continue with lowercase letters, digits, _ and -, up to 32 characters.

SEE ALSO
    login(1), passwd(1), useradd(1), id(1), index(7)
